import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/McpTemperatures
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The MCP package: a first server, what it answers (real requests, sent to it while the site is
## built), serving it over HTTP, who may ask, testing, what it speaks, its modules, and its status.
## Nothing here hydrates: the page is text and code, and its server's answers are the build's.
meta = {
	title: "MCP",
	description: "A Roc Solid app as an MCP server: its tools, resources and prompts, for AI agents. The protocol is plain Roc; an endpoint becomes a tool in a line, its schema the platform's.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "first", label: "A first server" },
		{ id: "answers", label: "What it answers" },
		{ id: "http", label: "Serving it over HTTP" },
		{ id: "access", label: "Who may ask" },
		{ id: "testing", label: "Testing a server" },
		{ id: "speaks", label: "What it speaks" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "status", label: "What is tested" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Limits" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<NoHydration>
		<PageHero
			kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/mcp</code>]}
			title="Your app's tools, for AI agents"
		>
			MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is how AI agents (and the apps they run in: Claude, an editor, the
			MCP Inspector) find out what an app can do, and do it. This package makes a Roc Solid app an MCP
			server. An endpoint the app already has becomes a tool in one line, and the tool's JSON Schema is
			the one the platform writes for <code>/openapi.json</code>, from the same Roc types.
		</PageHero>
		<p>
			Use it when you want an agent to work with your app's data: search it, read it, add to it. An MCP
			server offers three kinds of things:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Tools</strong>: actions the agent can call, with arguments, such as "create a note".</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Resources</strong>: data the agent can read by a URI, such as <code>notes://index</code>.
				A <strong>template</strong> is a family of resources whose URIs follow a pattern, such as
				<code>{"notes://note/{id}"}</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Prompts</strong>: ready-made messages a person picks from a menu in their agent (a slash
				command, say), filled in with the arguments they give.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The package has two parts. The protocol is plain Roc, on no platform, so you can test it with
			<code>expect</code>s. A small module on the server platform, <code>McpHttp</code>, serves it over
			HTTP and makes tools from your endpoints. The <a href={Paths.examples_notes}>notes example</a> is a
			full app that agents use this way.
		</p>

		<Heading id="first">A first server</Heading>
		<p>
			A server is a record: a name, a version, and what it offers. This one converts temperatures. Its
			tool's handler is a Roc function from a record to a record. The package reads the arguments an
			agent sends into the handler's argument type, and sends its answer back as JSON. When the handler
			returns <code>Err</code>, the tool refuses, and the model reads why.
		</p>
		<Code title={Sources.mcp_server.file} source={Sources.mcp_server.rocx} />
		<p>
			The JSON Schemas here are written by hand, because this page's server has no HTTP side. Over HTTP,
			<code>McpHttp.tool</code> writes them from the handler's types (<a href="#http">below</a>). The other
			fields:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<code>resources</code>: each is something an agent reads by its URI. <code>Resource.text</code>
				gives a URI, a name, a MIME type and a function that makes the text.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>templates</code>: each matches a family of URIs. In
				<code>{"temperatures://boiling/{substance}"}</code>, <code>{"{substance}"}</code> takes whatever the
				URI has in that place, and the read function is given it by name.
			</li>
			<li><code>prompts</code>: each makes messages from the arguments a person gives.</li>
		</ul>

		<Heading id="answers">What it answers</Heading>
		<p>
			This site is static files, so nothing here serves MCP. Instead, while the site was built, this page
			sent the server above the requests below, as a client would send them, and shows the package's
			answers as they came back (indented so they are easier to read).
		</p>
		{...McpTemperatures.exchanges!({}).map_with_index(
			|exchange, index|
				<details class="mcp-exchange" open?={index == 0}>
					<summary>{exchange.title}</summary>
					<div class="mcp-pair">
						<Code title="Request" source={exchange.request} numbers={Bool.False} />
						<Code title="Answer" source={exchange.answer} numbers={Bool.False} />
					</div>
				</details>,
		)}

		<Heading id="http">Serving it over HTTP</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>McpHttp</code> serves the MCP server at one path, such as <code>/mcp</code>. It sits behind the
			<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> that decides who may ask, and it makes tools
			from what the app already has: an <a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoint</a>, a function of
			the app's own, or a handler written for the tool. This sketch mounts it from <code>main.roc</code>
			and makes one tool of each kind:
		</p>
		<Code title="A sketch (this site serves no MCP): main.roc, and the server" source={Sources.mcp_http_sketch.rocx} />
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>McpHttp.</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>middleware(path, server)</code></td><td>The MCP server as a middleware. It answers requests to <code>path</code> and passes every other request on.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>respond!(server, request, context)</code></td><td>The answer to one request to the MCP route, for a route file's <code>respond!</code> if you'd rather serve it from <code>routes/mcp/+server.roc</code>.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>endpoint(Handlers.x, about, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>An endpoint as a tool. <code>Handlers.x</code> is the function from the endpoint's path params to the endpoint, in <code>Generated/Handlers</code>, which has each endpoint with its handler. The tool's arguments are one object holding both the path's params and the body (<code>{"{ \"id\": \"7\", \"tags\": [\"work\"] }"}</code>). The endpoint's answer is the tool's, and a refusal goes back to the model.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>function(Module.name!, about, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>A function of the app's own as a tool, run inside the MCP request, behind the MCP route's middleware.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>tool(about, handler!, McpHttp.openapi({"{}"}))</code></td><td>A handler written for the tool, <code>{"arg, Caller(c) => Try(result, Str)"}</code>, its schemas written from its types.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Caller(c)</code></td><td>What a handler is given: <code>{"{ request, context, progress! }"}</code>. <code>context</code> is what the middleware passed on, such as who is asking.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>About</code></td><td>A tool's <code>{"{ name, description }"}</code>, and optionally <code>title</code> and <code>tags</code>.</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>
		<p>
			<code>{"McpHttp.openapi({})"}</code> is always the same argument: the platform's schema writers,
			passed as a value. It is there because of a compiler limit: one generic function can't both read a
			type with <code>Json</code> and describe it with <code>OpenApi</code>, so the writers are passed in
			where the types are known.
		</p>
		<p>
			A tool marked <code>.reporting()</code> may call <code>progress!</code> to say how far it has got.
			When the client asks to hear progress, it gets the answer as a stream of
			<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>server-sent events</a>: each step as it is taken, then the answer.
		</p>
		<p>
			<code>McpHttp</code> is the package's own, on the server platform: an app names the package
			<code>mcp</code> in its header and writes <code>import mcp.McpHttp</code>. The browser's half of the app
			sees only the protocol's modules, which run anywhere.
		</p>

		<Heading id="access">Who may ask</Heading>
		<p>
			The middleware in front of the MCP route decides, as it does for any route. It can check a session
			cookie, an API key or an OAuth access token. The notes example checks a personal access token,
			which its users make on a settings page.
		</p>
		<p>
			MCP's own way is OAuth: a client without a good token gets a 401 that says where to learn how to
			get one. The <code>Bearer</code> module builds those answers:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>Bearer.token(headers)</code>: the token a request carries (<code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>).</li>
			<li><code>Bearer.challenge</code>: the 401, with a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header.</li>
			<li><code>Bearer.insufficient</code>: the 403 for a good token that lacks a scope.</li>
			<li>
				<code>Bearer.metadata</code>: the document a server serves at
				<code>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</code>.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Not yet: issuing tokens. The platform has no authorization server of its own; that is a proposal.
		</p>
		<p>
			Separately, the package refuses a request that a browser sends from a page of another site (by its
			<code>Origin</code> header). Without that, a web page could reach an MCP server running on the
			reader's own computer.
		</p>

		<Heading id="testing">Testing a server</Heading>
		<p>
			<code>Mcp.receive</code> is pure, so an app's <code>expect</code>s can send its server requests and
			read the answers, with no network. <code>Mcp.problems(server)</code> lists what MCP won't accept:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>a tool name that isn't 1 to 128 letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>.</code>;</li>
			<li>a name or a URI given twice;</li>
			<li>a schema that isn't JSON;</li>
			<li>an endpoint's tool whose path params and body have a field with the same name.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>The server on this page is tested this way:</p>
		<Code title={Sources.mcp_test.file} source={Sources.mcp_test.rocx} />

		<Heading id="speaks">What it speaks</Heading>
		<p>
			MCP over Streamable HTTP: one endpoint, where each POST carries one JSON-RPC message and is answered
			with JSON, or with a stream of events for a tool that reports progress. The same endpoint speaks
			both eras of the protocol:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>2026-07-28</strong>, the current version, has no handshake. Each request states its
				version and the client's capabilities in its <code>_meta</code>, and its headers repeat them
				(<code>MCP-Protocol-Version</code>, <code>Mcp-Method</code>, <code>Mcp-Name</code>).
				<code>server/discover</code> says what the server offers, and each list says how long a client may
				keep it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>2025-11-25, 2025-06-18 and 2025-03-26</strong> start with <code>initialize</code>, then
				<code>notifications/initialized</code>. The server keeps no session between requests, which these
				versions allow, so it sends no <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code>.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>A request</th><th>Is answered</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td>a notification (no id)</td><td>202, with an empty body</td></tr>
					<tr><td>a body that isn't JSON (by its <code>Content-Type</code>)</td><td>415</td></tr>
					<tr><td>not one JSON-RPC message (invalid JSON, a batch, no method)</td><td>400, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>a version the server doesn't speak, a header that doesn't match the body, or <code>_meta</code> missing what it needs</td><td>400, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>a method that doesn't exist (current MCP)</td><td>404, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>an unknown tool, a bad cursor, a resource that doesn't exist</td><td>200, a JSON-RPC error</td></tr>
					<tr><td>arguments that don't fit a tool, or a tool's refusal</td><td>200, a result the model reads (<code>isError</code>)</td></tr>
					<tr><td>from a page of another site (its <code>Origin</code>)</td><td>403</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>GET</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td><td>405: the server pushes nothing, and has no session to end</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>

		<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
		<p>
			The protocol reads a request, checks it, and either answers it or says what to run. It has no
			effects of its own: tools, resources and prompts are functions the app gives it.
			<code>roc test packages/mcp/main.roc</code> runs its <code>expect</code>s, including the spec's
			example messages. The package's README lists every function. What a tool is (<code>Tool</code>),
			what it answers (<code>Content</code>) and <code>JsonValue</code> are
			<a href={Paths.ecosystem_tools}>the tools package's</a>: a tool made there is served here as it is,
			and the AI package's models use it too. Name <code>tools</code> in your app's header beside
			<code>mcp</code>, and <code>import tools.Tool</code>.
		</p>
		<TableWrap>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>Mcp</code></td><td><code>Mcp.Server(ctx)</code>, what a server offers. <code>handle!(server, incoming, ctx)</code> answers a request. <code>receive</code> does the same purely: it returns an answer, or what to run. <code>problems(server)</code> is for a test.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Resource</code></td><td><code>Resource.text(uri, name, mime, read!)</code> and <code>Resource.new(uri, name, read!)</code>: what reading a URI gives. <code>Resource.contents</code> is text or bytes.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Template</code></td><td><code>Template.new(pattern, name, read!)</code>: a family of resources. <code>read!</code> gets the values the URI gave the pattern's names.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Prompt</code></td><td><code>Prompt.new(name, description, arguments, get!)</code>: messages made from string arguments (<code>Prompt.argument</code>, <code>Prompt.optional</code>, <code>Prompt.user</code>, <code>Prompt.assistant</code>).</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Bearer</code></td><td>The pieces of OAuth's answers, for the app's middleware (<a href="#access">above</a>).</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Rpc</code></td><td>JSON-RPC 2.0: <code>read</code> one message; write a <code>result</code>, an <code>error</code> or a <code>notification</code>.</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>
		<p>
			<code>Mcp.Server(ctx)</code> needs only <code>name</code> and <code>version</code>. The other fields
			have defaults:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>title</code>: the server's name for people;</li>
			<li><code>instructions</code>: for the model, how the tools fit together;</li>
			<li><code>tools</code>, <code>resources</code>, <code>templates</code>, <code>prompts</code>: none by default;</li>
			<li><code>page_size</code>: how many items go in one page of a list (100);</li>
			<li><code>ttl_ms</code>: how long a client may keep a list, in current MCP (5 minutes);</li>
			<li>
				<code>origins</code>: pages of other sites whose requests are answered. Pages of the server's own
				site, and requests from no page at all (an agent, <code>curl</code>), always are.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>The package has no components: its readers are agents, not people.</p>

		<Heading id="status">What is tested</Heading>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The protocol, in both eras, by 93 <code>expect</code>s (what a tool is has its own, in
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_tools}>the tools package</a>).
			</li>
			<li>
				Over HTTP, in an app built with <code>rocx build</code> on the server platform, with the official
				clients: the MCP Inspector's command line (2.16.0, which speaks 2025-11-25), and the TypeScript
				SDK's client (2.4.0) on 2026-07-28. That covers tools from an endpoint (with its path's params among
				the arguments), from a function and from a handler; refusals; resources; a template; a prompt; and a
				tool's progress, streamed as events.
			</li>
			<li>
				examples/notes, whose server is mounted from <code>main.roc</code> and whose tests call its tools
				over JSON-RPC. A route file with <code>respond!</code> alone can serve it too.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Waiting on a proposal (in <code>packages/mcp/PROPOSALS.md</code>):</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				a tag union in a tool's arguments, which the schema calls "any value" until the generated
				<code>Api</code> lists the tags it found;
			</li>
			<li>an endpoint's doc comment as its tool's description (today you repeat it);</li>
			<li>an OAuth authorization server of the platform's own.</li>
		</ul>

		<Heading id="limits">Limits</Heading>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Not yet: list-change notifications, resource subscriptions, completions, logging to the client,
				and requests from the server to the client (sampling, asking the user).
			</li>
			<li>No sessions: nothing is kept between requests.</li>
			<li>
				Every field of a typed tool's arguments is required, as the platform's schemas say. For an optional
				argument, use a <code>Tool.new</code> tool, which takes any JSON.
			</li>
			<li>A batch (a JSON array of messages) is refused: MCP has had no batches since 2025-06-18.</li>
		</ul>
		<details>
			<summary>This page's server, and the requests it was sent: <code>{Sources.mcp_demo.file}</code></summary>
			<Code title={Sources.mcp_demo.file} source={Sources.mcp_demo.rocx} />
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary>The HTTP side: <code>{Sources.mcp_http.file}</code></summary>
			<Code title={Sources.mcp_http.file} source={Sources.mcp_http.rocx} />
		</details>
	</NoHydration>

<style>
	/* A request and its answer, side by side where there is room. */
	.mcp-pair {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-4);
		align-items: start;
	}

	/* <Code>'s figure is that component's, so reached with :global. */
	.mcp-pair :global(.code) {
		margin: 0;
	}
</style>
